A||||17||burdell1||Transmission Slipping
Z||000000||burdell1||06-24-99||10:27 AM||||I'm having a problem with my automatic transmission when it goes into second gear. It used to jerk whenever it when into second before, however, after awhile my car had an exhaust manifold leak and it was not much of a problem then (it was so loud it was not noticeable) The other day I finally shelled out the money to get that fixed ($500!) and now it is a problem again. I read on the Fiero Online Service Guide that someone had a similar problem and it was a bad O ring seal on the intermediate band servo piston.  Does this sound right? Any other ideas?<br>
Z||000001||theogre||06-24-99||01:12 PM||||TH125c transmissions have a number of problems. Yes, the servo could be acting up. It could also be dirt, low fluid, a worn band, a bad clutch pack,,,,,,,,,,,,<p>There isn't much you can do to it without pulling it apart. You could try a cleaner in the thing, If it's dirt that sometimes helps.<p>If you end up pulling the tranny apart, replace the TCC lockup solinoid. That item is a known problem child, and it's not worth fixing the second gear problem, and not do that to.
Z||000002||burdell1||06-24-99||10:42 PM||||What are the symptoms of a bad TCC lockup solenoid? Is there anything having to do with the transmission that is connected near where the exhaust manifolds and intake manifolds are since that is what i got fixed recently&gt;
Z||000003||burdell1||06-24-99||10:42 PM||||What are the symptoms of a bad TCC lockup solenoid? Is there anything having to do with the transmission that is connected near where the exhaust manifolds and intake manifolds are since that is what i got fixed recently&gt;
Z||000004||Lynx||06-25-99||01:43 AM||||A bad TCC lockup solinoid will make the the car start jerking and die most of the time when you like get off the freeway and come to a stop and somtimes in town. Mainly when your at a high speed and come to a low speed quickly. (My moms Grand Am had this problem)
Z||000005||lowCG||06-25-99||02:12 AM||||Like the ogre wrote,it's a bunch of stuff in there,with a lot of miles on it;it's all going to be a little sloppy.<br>Does/did it shift consistantly,or soft sometimes and crisp others? There's several bushings that keep everything in line,when they wear, things aren't always spinning right where they should,and shift pressures will change sporadically.If it's banging hard,then somethings hanging up,like you read about,causing an abnormal(high)shift pressure.If it's only shifting firm,then maybe your modulator is disconnected,or faulty.<br>To take it to a decent shop for the regular maintenance/adjustments,which is not an expensive procedure,might be a good idea,and see what they say about it.Always get a second and third opinion for transmission problems to confirm what the other shops are finding.
Z||000006||burdell1||06-25-99||08:03 AM||||what it does is revs up when it's trying to go into gear, then when it does it has a bit of a jerk to it. I noticed it is doing this in third gear as well, but not nearly as much. it is very consistent...it does it every time. <br>
Z||000007||theogre||06-25-99||08:24 AM||||did you check the fluid level?<p>TH125c doesn't use a vacuum modulator. It uses a cable to the throtle. The cable is commonly called the kick down cable becuse most people think it just makes the thing drop into second when you floor it, but that's not the only thing it does in this tranny. Make sure that cable is operating freely or you'll get some strange problems.<p>If those 2 things are good, then you have some type of problem that is likely going to mean the tranny has to come appart.
Z||000008||burdell1||06-25-99||07:24 PM||||I checked the fluid level and it is fine. Where do I check the kick down cable? 
Z||000009||burdell1||06-25-99||08:01 PM||||Is it safe to put some cleaning solutions in the transmission fluid? I've heard that it is a bad idea to add things to the motor oil but wasn't sure about the transmission.
Z||000010||theogre||06-25-99||10:00 PM||||the TV cable comes right out the top of the tranny. where it goes from there depends on motor, and year. just follow it and see where it goes.<p>DO NOT put solvents into anything!!!!! I ment use one of the better tranny aditive products. These do a combination of cleaning small dirt, and contail plastisizers to improve the condition of some seals.<p>I mentioned the TCC solinoid Only because if the tranny is disassembled for any reason this part should be changed while you are there. It's a cheap part, and good insurance. You would also replace the filter at that time.<p>The best repair for TH125 is to have it profesionally rebuilt. the next best is a Known Good used tranny. I strongly recomend you DO NOT disassemble the tranny yourself unless you know exactly what you are doing. While nearly everyone here could safely change the filter, or fix a pan leak, all other work is extreamly precise and easy to make major messes out of.<p>A problem like this that is happening in both shift points could be any number of things. Odds are the tranny has allot of miles on it. finding a low milage unit from a more recent year would probably be the cheapest fix but I don't know if front wheel drive TH125's have the same shift lever or not. (I'm talking about the lever on the tranny.) I think the lever is relativly easy to change, but I don't feel like going out to the car in the dark to look and make sure.
Z||000011||lowCG||06-25-99||10:22 PM||||Is the fluid red,or brownish-red(brown)?<br>Have you ever changed out the fluid and filter(just under the pan on the bottom)?<br>Would probably be worth a try if you've never done before.<br>The fluid's job is to transfer power,and keep the little clutches from burning up;like the ogre wrote,don't add anything that'll help burn up the well worn clutches.<br>Dirty fluid won't dissapate  heat that well either.
Z||000012||burdell1||06-26-99||08:29 AM||||I wasn't planning on putting any solvents in the transmission fluid. I was talking about those transmission additive products. I've heard that it is not a good idea to add stuff to your motor oil, but the transmission I wasn't sure about. What is the best one out there or doesn't it make a difference?<p>
Z||000013||theogre||06-26-99||10:55 AM||||If the fluid is brown, and worse if it smells burnt, No additive will help.<p>I don't use these type of things very often. Sometimes they help. sometimes they don't. I haven't seen any actually cause damage yet, but like I said, I haven't used that many.<p>One I had gotten recently for my minivan, but didn't use because the van's gone now, is K&W Trans-X. They at least have a "satisfaction garantee" and an 800 phone number. The product is one of the few I've seen that isn't wrapped in hype, just a rather plain bilingual can.<p>Before adding a product of any kind you must reduce the fluid by the same amount as the additive you are putting in. Over filling a tranny can cause problems.
Z||000014||burdell1||06-26-99||12:46 PM||||I'm not sure if this information helps, but the transmission goes into second gear when the RPMs reach 3500.<p>
Z||000015||burdell1||06-28-99||10:39 AM||||O.K. This is getting really weird. All of a sudden, out of nowhere my car starts running fine. The only thing that it was still doing was a little jerk when i start to slow down (which it has done for what seems like forever.) However, the next day it started up again. I adjusted the kickdown cable like you said, but that did not work right away, but I think it might have adjusted itself just by slowing down and speeding up. also, the thin wires going to the throttle seem to be a little frayed. Will that do it? If I were to buy a new one, would those wires come with that cable?<br>
Z||000016||theogre||06-28-99||02:16 PM||||yes a worn cable could cause problems. the cable is replaced as an assembly. It may still be available thru Pontiac/GM dealers. The same cable fits multiple cars.
Z||000017||burdell1||06-30-99||08:30 PM||||Is it very difficult to replace the TV cable? Or is it something that I shouldn't do myself and leave for a mechanic?<br> 
